### 3. Data Modeling Strategies for 5 TB+ Tables

#### Strategy 1: Pre-Join at Silver (Eliminate Gold-Layer Shuffles)

The #1 mistake with large tables is joining them at the gold layer every run.

```
❌ BAD: Join 5 TB fact + dimensions every time gold refreshes
   gold.summary = JOIN(silver.transactions[5TB], silver.products, silver.stores)
   → 5 TB shuffle every single run

✅ GOOD: Pre-join once at silver, gold reads pre-joined table
   silver.transactions_enriched = JOIN(silver.transactions[5TB], products, stores)
   → Only new/changed data joined (incremental)
   gold.summary = AGGREGATE(silver.transactions_enriched)
   → Gold reads pre-joined data, no shuffle on 5 TB
```

**Implementation:**
```sql
-- Silver intermediate: pre-join fact + dimensions (run daily, incremental)
CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE silver.transactions_enriched
CLUSTER BY (transaction_date, category_id, region)
AS SELECT
    t.*,
    p.product_name, p.category, p.subcategory, p.brand,
    s.store_name, s.region, s.state, s.format
FROM STREAM silver.transactions t
JOIN silver.products_current p ON t.product_id = p.product_id
JOIN silver.stores_current s ON t.store_id = s.store_id;
```

Now gold aggregations are simple scans with no joins:
```sql
CREATE OR REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW gold.daily_category_sales
AS SELECT
    transaction_date,
    region,
    category,
    SUM(amount) AS revenue,
    COUNT(*) AS transaction_count
FROM silver.transactions_enriched
GROUP BY transaction_date, region, category;
```

#### Strategy 2: Star Schema with Satellite Tables

For very wide fact tables (100+ columns), split into core fact + satellite tables:

```
┌───────────────────────┐
│  CORE FACT TABLE      │     ┌── SATELLITE: Payment Details ──┐
│  (transaction_id PK)  │     │  transaction_id (FK)           │
│  transaction_date     ├────►│  payment_method                │
│  store_id (FK)        │     │  card_type, auth_code          │
│  product_id (FK)      │     └────────────────────────────────┘
│  customer_id (FK)     │
│  amount               │     ┌── SATELLITE: Loyalty Details ──┐
│  quantity             ├────►│  transaction_id (FK)           │
│  5 TB, 20 columns     │     │  points_earned, tier           │
│                       │     │  coupon_code, discount          │
└───────┬───────────────┘     └────────────────────────────────┘
        │
        │ FK joins
  ┌─────▼─────┐  ┌──────────┐  ┌───────────┐
  │ Products  │  │  Stores  │  │ Customers │
  │ 50 MB     │  │  5 MB    │  │  2 GB     │
  └───────────┘  └──────────┘  └───────────┘
```

**Why:** Queries that only need amount + product_name scan 5 TB x 20 columns = much less I/O than 5 TB x 100 columns. Satellite tables are joined only when those specific columns are needed.

#### Strategy 3: Aggregate Tables at Multiple Granularities

Don't make dashboards scan 5 TB. Pre-aggregate at the granularity each consumer needs:

| Aggregate Table | Granularity | Size | Refreshed | Consumers |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `gold.hourly_store_sales` | Hour x Store x Category | \~50 GB | Hourly | Operational dashboards |
| `gold.daily_region_sales` | Day x Region x Category | \~5 GB | Daily | Management dashboards |
| `gold.weekly_brand_perf` | Week x Brand x Region | \~500 MB | Weekly | Strategic reports |
| `gold.monthly_kpis` | Month x Business Unit | \~50 MB | Monthly | Executive summary |

```sql
-- Hourly aggregate (operational)
CREATE OR REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW gold.hourly_store_sales
AS SELECT
    date_trunc('HOUR', transaction_timestamp) AS hour,
    store_id, category,
    SUM(amount) AS revenue,
    COUNT(*) AS txn_count,
    COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) AS unique_customers
FROM silver.transactions_enriched
WHERE transaction_date >= current_date() - 7  -- Rolling 7 days only
GROUP BY 1, 2, 3;
```

